#!/usr/bin/env -S uv run --script
# /// script
# requires-python = ">=3.11"
# dependencies = []
# ///
r"""End-to-end throughput benchmark of a 3-node espresso-node network, for regression tracking.

`run` starts scripts/network-bench/process-compose.yaml (3 equal-stake validators, so every
certificate needs all 3; node0 on Postgres with the query service, node1 and node2 on SQLite),
drives it with a paced load generator, samples node metrics and host CPU, tears the network
down, and writes `result.json` plus a markdown `summary.md` into the output directory.

Load is a staircase: after a warmup, `tx_size` transactions go out evenly spaced at each rate
of `steps` for `step_s`, round-robin over the first `submit_nodes` nodes, independent of
inclusion; `cap_s` of load caps the transactions in flight. Each step is judged on its second
half: it fails if decided falls behind the offered rate, a view times out,
consensus latency (submit to the block on a validator) exceeds its target, or the query node's
lag behind the validators exceeds its target or grows. At the first failing step the ramp stops
and one refine step runs halfway back. Capacity is the highest passing rate, split into the
consensus and query node limits. Each transaction carries a per-run marker and its id, so
inclusion is found by scanning node0's block payloads, one fetch per block.

A run is `noisy` when steal time, calibration drift, submit errors, lost payloads or a lagging
payload scan suggest the runner, not the code, moved the numbers; noisy runs are kept but never
used as a baseline.

Exit codes:
    0  the run is valid, whatever the performance deltas
    1  the run is invalid (network not ready, a node stalled, no transactions included, ...)
    2  preflight refused to start (leftover processes or busy ports)

Examples:
    scripts/network-bench/bench run --bin-dir target/release --out bench-out
    scripts/network-bench/bench render bench-out --baseline main-network-bench.json
    scripts/network-bench/bench compare bench-out/result.json --baseline other/result.json
    scripts/network-bench/bench sysinfo --out bench-out
"""

